  "id": "chart-fitted-line-dinner-glucose",
  "type": "scatter",
  "title": "The least-squares line through 60 people: fasting glucose against the dinner-to-sleep interval",
  "alt": "Scatter of 60 people with a straight fitted line sloping gently downwards from left to right, from about 151 mg/dL at 0.8 hours to about 131 mg/dL at 6.5 hours.",
  "longDescription": "The 60 dots are the same 60 people plotted earlier in this block: horizontal position is the interval between dinner and sleep in hours, vertical position is next-morning fasting glucose in mg/dL. The straight line drawn over them is the least-squares line fitted to exactly these 60 pairs. Its equation is fitted glucose = 153.4 - 3.37 x interval, with glucose in mg/dL and the interval in hours. The slope is -3.37 mg/dL per hour: each extra hour between dinner and sleep goes with a fitted glucose 3.37 mg/dL lower, and the sign is negative because a later dinner leaves a shorter interval. The intercept is 153.4 mg/dL, the value the line gives at an interval of zero, which is outside the data. The correlation is r = -0.32 and r squared is 0.10. The line is drawn only from 0.8 to 6.5 hours, the smallest and largest intervals actually recorded, and it passes through the point at 3.375 hours and 142.0 mg/dL, the mean of each measurement. The dots stay widely scattered above and below the line at every interval, so the line describes the drift of the cloud and not any one person's reading.",
